# Search provenance owned by the search result

## Problem

`west mid` matches a funder alias but the result shows only `Unite Union`. The repository currently returns the maximum canonical/alias score, and every later layer loses its origin. Preserve canonical identity while carrying the winning source. Keep exact word_similarity scores, inclusive request strictness, independent entity limits, one result per funder, and existing MP/funder merge behavior. Add the three requested GiST indexes without claiming that ordinary function comparisons use them.

## Usage (caller's view)

Repository caller:

```rust
let funders: Vec<(FunderSearchMatch, SearchSimilarity)> =
    repo.get_funders_by_text_search_score(&request).await?;
// Sorted, thresholded, limited funder identities; winning source travels with identity.
```

HTTP conversion:

```rust
match entity {
    Entity::Funder(hit) => {
        // identity/name/kind from hit.funder(); matched_by from hit.matched_by()
    }
    Entity::ParliamentMember(member) => {
        // identity/name from member; matched_by = SearchMatchSource::Name
    }
}
```

UI:

```svelte
<a href={entityHref(entity)} class="entity-name">{entity.name}</a>
{#if entity.matchedBy.kind === "alias"}
  <span class="matched-alias">Matched alias: {entity.matchedBy.name}</span>
{/if}
```

Keep existing highlightName segments for both displayed names. Never use raw HTML.

## Shape

Extend `domain/models/entity_search.rs`, the existing search domain, rather than changing `Funder` details or introducing another service.

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize)]
#[serde(tag = "kind", rename_all = "snake_case")]
pub enum SearchMatchSource {
    Name,
    Alias { name: String },
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct FunderSearchMatch {
    funder: Funder,
    matched_by: SearchMatchSource,
}
impl FunderSearchMatch {
    pub fn new(funder: Funder, matched_by: SearchMatchSource) -> Self { todo!() }
    pub fn funder(&self) -> &Funder { todo!() }
    pub fn matched_by(&self) -> &SearchMatchSource { todo!() }
}

pub enum Entity {
    Funder(FunderSearchMatch),
    ParliamentMember(ParliamentMember),
}
```

`FunderSearchMatch` owns the association between the identity and its query-specific source. `SearchMatchSource` makes an alias payload compulsory. `Entity` prevents MPs from acquiring alias metadata. `Funder` remains reusable outside search. Existing Entity getters delegate through the funder hit. Replace `From<&Funder>` with `From<&FunderSearchMatch>`; do not retain a compatibility route that silently assigns Name. ParliamentMemberRepo retains its tuple return type and its ingestion callers are untouched. FunderRepo replaces only its tuple's first element. The merge function changes its funder input and otherwise preserves its two-pointer comparison, including the present alternating MP/funder behavior on equal scores. EntitySearchService still returns Vec<Entity>.

At the SQL boundary, `matched_alias: Option<String>` is a storage representation; map None to Name and Some to Alias immediately. HTTP SearchEntity adds `matched_by: SearchMatchSource` using the canonical enum's serde encoding. No separately maintained string enum or response mapper for the source vocabulary. This concentrates query policy in the repository and exposes just the domain outcome, per boundary-discipline and minimize-reader-load.

Wire examples:

```json
{"id":"…","name":"Unite Union","kind":"Funder","funder_kind":"Trade Union","matched_by":{"kind":"alias","name":"West Midlands Unite"}}
{"id":"123","name":"John Smith","kind":"MP","funder_kind":null,"matched_by":{"kind":"name"}}
```

Frontend uses `SearchMatchSource = {kind:"name"} | {kind:"alias"; name:string}`. Funder entities carry either source; MP entity matchedBy has the name-only subtype. Parse unknown JSON in the existing readEntities boundary, reject absent/unknown source kinds, missing/non-string alias name, and an alias source for MP. Update fixtures with the new required field in this change. Do not guess Name from missing metadata. The backend and frontend ship as one contract here.

## Query contract

The smallest exact query replaces repeated correlated MAX calculations with one best-alias lateral subquery. Use sqlx::query! as today:

```sql
SELECT f.id, f.funder_name AS name, f.funder_kind AS kind,
       GREATEST(n.score, COALESCE(a.score, 0::real)) AS similarity_score,
       CASE WHEN a.score > n.score THEN a.funder_alias END AS matched_alias
FROM exposed.funders f
CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
  SELECT word_similarity($1, f.funder_name) AS score
) n
LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
  SELECT fa.funder_alias, word_similarity($1, fa.funder_alias) AS score
  FROM exposed.funder_aliases fa
  WHERE fa.funder_id = f.id
  ORDER BY score DESC, fa.funder_alias COLLATE "C" ASC
  LIMIT 1
) a ON TRUE
WHERE GREATEST(n.score, COALESCE(a.score, 0::real)) >= $2
ORDER BY similarity_score DESC, f.id ASC
LIMIT $3
```

Pass i64::from(req.max_entries()) as $3 and remove rank/take_while. Alias lexical ties have deterministic byte order. A strict `>` assigns equal canonical/alias scores to canonical. No aliases gives canonical. Zero threshold still returns zero-score rows; threshold one remains inclusive. LIMIT applies after one row per funder, so alias multiplicity cannot consume identity slots. Retain the exact score value and add deterministic identity ordering for same-score rows. Apply the corresponding `ORDER BY similarity_score DESC, parliament_member_id ASC LIMIT $3` cleanup to member SQL while keeping its tuple API.

Three baseline indexes:

```sql
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS members_name_trgm_gist_idx
  ON exposed.members USING gist (name exposed.gist_trgm_ops);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS funders_name_trgm_gist_idx
  ON exposed.funders USING gist (funder_name exposed.gist_trgm_ops);
CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS funder_aliases_alias_trgm_gist_idx
  ON exposed.funder_aliases USING gist (funder_alias exposed.gist_trgm_ops);
```

Fold these into the sole baseline up migration, after their tables. Down table drops remove associated indexes; explicit drops are optional if following current down style. For live DB apply these idempotent statements only after inspecting existing definitions, then schema-verify before checksum repair per db/README.md. IF NOT EXISTS does not verify an existing index definition.

### Honest GiST applicability

This exact aggregate query does not become index-backed merely because indexes exist. Function comparisons `word_similarity(...) >= $2` do not use GiST. The indexes support pg_trgm threshold operators and KNN word distance `column <->> query`. A read-only EXPLAIN of `SELECT ... FROM exposed.funder_aliases ORDER BY funder_alias <->> 'west mid' LIMIT 10` can establish the expected KNN access path on realistic data, but is not evidence that the application's grouped funder query uses it. Do not claim a runtime speedup.

I recommend shipping requested indexes and provenance with this exact query. If the accepted task requires the application's search itself to exploit GiST, this candidate needs a deliberate second design decision before implementation. Changing all ranking to KNN distance can collapse distinct real scores through subtraction rounding; limiting alias rows before identity dedup can omit valid funders. Those are correctness costs, not harmless optimization details. Avoid a session threshold GUC entirely in this small change.

## Verification

SQLx database tests should insert one funder with many matching aliases plus several distinct funders. Check identity limit is filled, each identity occurs once, source follows the highest score, original alias spelling survives, canonical wins equal scores, equal alias scores choose C lexical order, and strictness 0/1 plus an exact intermediate score are inclusive. Include a direct canonical match whose aliases score lower. Compare returned scores/IDs to a simple exhaustive reference query on the fixture. This catches candidate pruning errors more directly than an index-name assertion.

Service tests must assert alias metadata survives mixed MP/funder ranking, including ties and empty sides. HTTP serialization test should assert the exact Name/Alias wire shapes. Frontend Playwright should consume an actual serialized fixture or matching golden shape, render canonical link with alias explanation, verify direct-name rows remain compact, preserve URL query/strictness navigation, reject malformed metadata, and display hostile alias markup literally. Check a long alias at a narrow viewport; wrap within the name column, avoid overflow, associate secondary text through aria-describedby on the canonical link if it sits outside the link. Keyboard focus remains one link per result.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept the existing funder scan in exchange for exact scoring and a small reviewable change. The three requested indexes are useful access paths, but not a claimed optimization of this grouped query.
- We accept modifying search-only Entity's funder payload in exchange for making member-alias combinations impossible and preserving the service signature.
- We accept a required additional JSON field in exchange for preventing old or malformed producers from falsely implying canonical matches.

## Alternatives considered

A generic `SearchHit<T> {entity:T,score,source}` used by both repositories would unify merge code, but changes ParliamentMemberRepo's ingestion consumers and permits aliases on MPs unless more type machinery is added. It hides scoring metadata while exposing an unnecessarily broad generic abstraction.

A materialized search-name table with one canonical/alias row per identity would give one GiST search vocabulary and simplify future KNN retrieval. It introduces another state writer and invalidation rules on ingestion/loading, and still requires correct identity dedup before limit. Its broader ownership and mutation cost outweigh this read-only change.

A transaction-local word_similarity_threshold plus index operators can prune candidates before exact filtering, but has strict/inclusive boundary and zero-threshold traps. It exposes connection-state policy in an otherwise simple search repository and needs more proof than this request warrants.

## Synthesis decision

Pending parent arena comparison. Preserve the exact-score query and domain-owned source enum even if a better proven GiST query strategy is grafted in.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation performed; source and AGENTS read in the designated task worktree. Artifact only is written.

## Open questions and risks

Does the accepted user scope require demonstrated application-query GiST usage, or only the requested indexes? Parent can resolve from the original request without stopping for another user confirmation. If index usage is mandatory, reject this candidate's conservative query rather than report an optimization it does not supply.

## Next implementation step

Introduce SearchMatchSource and FunderSearchMatch, update the repository return contract and SQL together, then carry the result through HTTP and frontend with the behavior tests above.

### Late grounding evidence from parent

Parent verified read-only PostgreSQL 18 transaction-local threshold behavior: `<%` includes exact score 1 at threshold 1 and score 0 at threshold 0. This removes the hypothesized endpoint-exclusion concern for the observed database. A transaction-local operator prefilter can therefore be a practical index-backed alternative without an epsilon, provided each repository's query and `set_config(..., true)` share the same transaction and exact `>= $2` remains the final authority. Avoid deriving exact ranking from rounded KNN distance. Parent reproduced `west mid` at strictness 0.6 returning canonical `Unite the Union` through aliases at score 0.8888889.
# Alias-aware search with an explicit search result

## Problem

Searching `west mid` can correctly return canonical `Unite Union`, but the service discards why it matched. Preserve canonical identity, scores, inclusive strictness and the independent per-kind limit; add the requested GiST indexes without silently replacing exact word similarity with approximate candidate truncation. Current funder queries calculate the same maximum three times. ParliamentMemberRepo has ingestion callers, so changing its existing result unnecessarily widens the task.

## Usage (caller's view)

```rust
// HTTP boundary keeps the existing request parsing and response envelope.
let matches = service.search_entities(&request).await?;
let response = SearchEntityResponseData::from(matches.as_slice());

// Service consumes funder provenance, retaining the existing MP repository contract.
let funders = repo.get_funders_by_text_search_score(&request).await?;
let members = mp_repo.get_members_by_text_search_score(&request).await?;
// Merge by score with existing MP-first cross-kind tie behavior.
```

```ts
const entities = await searchEntities(term, signal, strictness);
// Canonical name and canonical link remain the result's primary identity.
if (entity.matchSource.kind === "alias") {
  // Render ordinary escaped text: Matched alias: {entity.matchSource.alias}
}
```

## Shape

In `domain/models/entity_search.rs`, use search-only records. Keep Funder untouched. A distinct funder hit guarantees ParliamentMember cannot acquire alias provenance; the common outward match uses the existing Entity identity enum.

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, serde::Serialize)]
#[serde(tag = "kind", rename_all = "snake_case")]
pub enum MatchSource {
    Name,
    Alias { alias: String },
}

pub struct FunderSearchHit {
    pub funder: Funder,
    pub score: SearchSimilarity,
    pub source: MatchSource,
}

pub struct EntitySearchHit {
    entity: Entity,
    source: MatchSource,
}
impl EntitySearchHit {
    // Existing Entity contains identity; constructors control which source is possible.
    pub fn member(member: ParliamentMember) -> Self { todo!() }
    pub fn funder(hit: FunderSearchHit) -> Self { todo!() }
    pub fn entity(&self) -> &Entity { todo!() }
    pub fn source(&self) -> &MatchSource { todo!() }
}

// Existing FunderRepo search method changes to Vec<FunderSearchHit>.
// ParliamentMemberRepo remains Vec<(ParliamentMember, SearchSimilarity)>.
// EntitySearchService search_entities changes to Vec<EntitySearchHit>.
fn merge_scores(
    members: &[(ParliamentMember, SearchSimilarity)],
    funders: &[FunderSearchHit],
) -> Vec<EntitySearchHit> { todo!() }
```

MatchSource is the canonical closed vocabulary and serializes directly inside the HTTP DTO, per boundary-discipline. Its alias field exists only for Alias. The SQL repository owns score/source selection because it reads the stored names and computes their scores. Keep the public shape shallow: callers see canonical identity and one explanation; neither ranking SQL nor alias collections escape the repository. For stronger restriction without additional types, make FunderSearchHit fields private and expose its constructor/getters only as required by the existing repo style.

### Query contract

Use one SQLx `query!` with a lateral best-alias selection, then calculate winning score/source once in a subquery. The essential SQL is:

```sql
SELECT scored.*
FROM (
  SELECT f.id, f.funder_name AS name, f.funder_kind AS kind,
         GREATEST(word_similarity($1, f.funder_name),
                  COALESCE(a.alias_score, 0)) AS similarity_score,
         CASE WHEN a.alias_score > word_similarity($1, f.funder_name)
              THEN a.funder_alias ELSE NULL END AS matched_alias
  FROM exposed.funders f
  LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.funder_alias, word_similarity($1, a.funder_alias) AS alias_score
    FROM exposed.funder_aliases a WHERE a.funder_id = f.id
    ORDER BY alias_score DESC, a.funder_alias COLLATE "C" ASC
    LIMIT 1
  ) a ON TRUE
) scored
WHERE scored.similarity_score >= $2
ORDER BY scored.similarity_score DESC, scored.name COLLATE "C" ASC, scored.id ASC
LIMIT $3
```

Decode matched_alias NULL as Name, otherwise Alias with original spelling. Alias must win strictly; ties select canonical. The lateral LIMIT selects one alias per identity rather than limiting raw aliases before deduplication. Push the identity limit into SQL instead of fetching all rows and using row_number/take_while. Apply the same deterministic name/ID tie ordering and SQL LIMIT to the MP query. Keep word_similarity as the scoring operation, including at strictness 0 and 1; preserve MP-first cross-kind ties in the merge.

### GiST applicability

The parent's read-only PostgreSQL 18 probe confirms exact-score-one matches pass operator threshold 1 and zero-score matches pass threshold 0. Therefore the recommended implementation can use a simple transaction-local prefilter without epsilon arithmetic: begin a repository transaction, call SQLx `query!` with `SELECT set_config('pg_trgm.word_similarity_threshold', $1, true)` using request strictness, select candidate IDs via indexed `funder_name %> $term UNION SELECT funder_id FROM funder_aliases WHERE funder_alias %> $term`, and join those IDs to the exact scoring query above. Apply `members.name %> $term` similarly. Keep the exact inclusive function comparison as final authority, and commit the read transaction before returning. UNION deduplicates identities before LIMIT. Repository owns this scope; callers and pooled connection state remain unaffected. Use actual database regression checks at 0/1 and exact interior score before adopting this path.

Add indexes on `exposed.members(name exposed.gist_trgm_ops)`, `exposed.funders(funder_name exposed.gist_trgm_ops)`, and `exposed.funder_aliases(funder_alias exposed.gist_trgm_ops)` using `USING gist`. Add them to the existing reversible baseline, with repeatable in-place upgrade SQL and the documented schema verification/checksum procedure for populated databases.

Be precise: this exact-score query does not become index-backed merely because these indexes exist. pg_trgm GiST accelerates its matching operators and KNN distance ordering, while a word_similarity function comparison is not an index condition. The recommended transaction-local operator candidate path gives these indexes genuine applicability; the exact scoring SQL alone remains a useful reference oracle. Alias KNN truncation before dedup can lose funders; distance rounding also must not become the authority for canonical-vs-alias preference. If actual indexed execution is a requirement, treat that as an explicit implementation acceptance criterion and prefer the alternative below rather than pretending the above query meets it.

### HTTP and frontend contract

Extend SearchEntity DTO by `match_source: MatchSource`; retain id,name,kind,funder_kind verbatim. Name JSON is `{ "kind": "name" }`; alias JSON is `{ "kind": "alias", "alias": "West Midlands Unite…" }`. Serialize the canonical enum rather than another string vocabulary. Frontend declares `MatchSource = {kind:"name"} | {kind:"alias";alias:string}` and parses unknown JSON exhaustively in readEntities, rejecting missing/unknown kind, alias without text, and MP+alias. Keep API order. Update controlled Playwright responses to the new contract.

Wrap each name link and optional alias in a text column inside entity-row. Show the alias below the canonical link with `overflow-wrap:anywhere`; keep icons/kind in existing columns. Render alias via Svelte interpolation, optionally existing highlightName segments. Link describes canonical entity; associate visible alias with the link using aria-describedby for keyboard/screen-reader users. Direct name hits gain no extra row content.

### Meaningful verification

- SQLx isolated database: canonical-only, alias-only `west mid`, both tied => Name, two aliases tied => lexical original alias; no aliases and strictness=0; score exactly threshold and strictness=1; many aliases belonging to one funder cannot consume identity limit; deterministic repeated ties; MP and funder ordering preserved.
- API contract check serializes real Name/Alias and verifies full expected payload; frontend controlled response accepts both and rejects unknown/malformed provenance.
- Playwright: alias result shows canonical link and visible secondary alias, canonical result lacks secondary label, click retains canonical route, hostile alias text renders literally, long alias wraps at narrow viewport and is associated with focused link.
- Inspect installed index definitions/opclasses in both fresh test schema and live upgrade; verify live row counts/schema before checksum sync. No benchmark or speedup assertion.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate proposal; parent selects the base and records adopted/rejected parts.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept a small search-specific wrapper in exchange for preserving Entity/Funder identity boundaries and ingestion MP repository callers.
- We accept one read transaction and transaction-local setting per repository search in exchange for genuine index applicability with exact threshold/dedup/tie semantics.
- We accept one new required JSON field in exchange for a contract that cannot silently lose provenance; update backend and frontend together.

## Alternatives considered

One unified search repository returns already-ranked EntitySearchHit rows from a canonical-name/alias union. It hides merging and provenance behind an even deeper interface, but loses the existing per-repository service abstraction and forces conversion of different entity columns in one adapter; too broad for this task.

Exact-score-only scan with installed GiST indexes: smallest implementation and fully preserves current semantics, but does not actually use the requested index capability in current search. Rejected as the base once the parent verified the operator's inclusive boundary semantics; retain it as the test oracle. Avoid epsilon-adjusted dynamic thresholds, KNN oversampling and alias pre-limit heuristics, which expose unnecessary correctness obligations.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation yet. Reconcile accepted deviations in this artifact before handoff.

## Open questions and risks

Do PostgreSQL versions supported by this project preserve the empirically verified inclusive operator semantics? Regression tests settle this; the function comparison remains the scoring authority. Can live migration run inside the existing documented maintenance approach while preserving imported records?

## Next implementation step

Add the MatchSource/search hit types, update funder query and service merge/HTTP contract together, then implement frontend parsing and secondary alias rendering.
## Problem

Search already scores a funder as the maximum `word_similarity(query, canonical_name)` or `word_similarity(query, alias)`, applies the inclusive request threshold, limits MPs and funders independently, then merges both ranked lists. The service currently drops scores and source names, so HTTP cannot explain why an alias found a canonical funder. GiST indexes need an indexable trigram operator predicate to participate; merely indexing columns under the existing function/aggregate predicates does not make those predicates indexable. Preserve search ordering, threshold inclusivity (including 0 and 1), per-kind limits, and canonical-name tie preference while exposing a typed match source.

## Usage (caller's view)

The service returns ranked results whose entity remains the canonical identity and whose source is explicit:

```rust
let results: Vec<EntitySearchResult> = search.search_entities(&request).await?;
for result in results {
    match &result.source {
        MatchSource::FunderAlias(alias) => show_alias_hint(result.entity.name(), alias),
        MatchSource::CanonicalName | MatchSource::MemberName => {}
    }
}
```

Repository result construction is similarly explicit:

```rust
let funders: Vec<ScoredFunder> = funder_repo.search_funders(&request).await?;
// Each row carries canonical Funder, exact winning score, and winning source.
```

The HTTP conversion emits an alias source only when the winning alias score is strictly greater than the canonical score. The browser parses the source as a closed discriminated union and shows `Matched alias: …` below the canonical result link only for the alias variant.

## Shape

### Types and signatures

Place search result types beside `Entity` in `domain/models/entity_search.rs`; do not add provenance to `Funder`, which is an identity/details type.

```rust
#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum MatchSource {
    CanonicalName,
    FunderAlias(String),
    MemberName,
}

#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq)]
pub struct EntitySearchResult {
    entity: Entity,
    similarity: SearchSimilarity,
    source: MatchSource,
}

impl EntitySearchResult {
    pub fn entity(&self) -> &Entity;
    pub fn similarity(&self) -> SearchSimilarity;
    pub fn source(&self) -> &MatchSource;
}

pub struct ScoredFunder {
    funder: Funder,
    similarity: SearchSimilarity,
    source: MatchSource, // CanonicalName or FunderAlias(alias); never MemberName
}

trait FunderRepo {
    fn get_funders_by_text_search_score(
        &self, req: &EntitySearchRequest,
    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Vec<ScoredFunder>, FunderRepoError>> + Send;
}

// Keep the MP repository API and its other ingestion/loading callers intact.
trait ParliamentMemberRepo {
    fn get_members_by_text_search_score(
        &self, req: &EntitySearchRequest,
    ) -> impl Future<Output = Result<Vec<(ParliamentMember, SearchSimilarity)>, ParliamentMemberRepoError>> + Send;
}

trait EntitySearchService {
    async fn search_entities(
        &self, req: &EntitySearchRequest,
    ) -> Result<Vec<EntitySearchResult>, EntitySearchError>;
}
```

`merge_scores` should merge the existing descending sequences while preserving each score and source; MP rows become `EntitySearchResult { source: MemberName }`. Use stable deterministic secondary order (existing repository order for MP/funder ties, and funder ID as final DB tie-break) without changing the current MP-before-funder equality behavior.

### Indexed query

Add GiST indexes using the extension schema's opclass on `exposed.members.name`, `exposed.funders.funder_name`, and `exposed.funder_aliases.funder_alias`. Existing composite alias PK remains useful for funder lookup but does not replace the alias-text GiST index.

The index applies to PostgreSQL's `word_similarity` trigram operators (`query <<% indexed_text`), not to `word_similarity(...) >= $threshold` alone. In a transaction, set `pg_trgm.word_similarity_threshold` locally to a small fixed margin below the requested threshold (clamp to zero), then use `query <<% column` as a candidate predicate and retain the exact `word_similarity(query, column) >= $threshold` predicate in the final query. This margin deliberately yields a superset; exact filtering preserves inclusive semantics at equality. At strictness 0, omit the candidate operator because its strict `>` semantics would lose zero-score rows; scan/filter exactly. Margin is a fixed storage-precision cushion, not a tunable second search threshold. Transaction-local setting avoids connection-pool leakage. At strictness 1, the cushion admits exact matches for the final inclusive filter.

For funders, use an indexable candidate CTE for canonical rows and another for alias rows. Score each candidate with the exact function, then group by funder and select its winner ordered by score descending, source rank (canonical first), alias text ascending. Apply `score >= req.strictness()` after the winner is chosen, order by score descending then funder ID, and apply `LIMIT req.max_entries()` in SQL. This carries the exact winning alias spelling. The GiST alias index finds candidate alias rows; do not globally limit alias rows before grouping, because many aliases may belong to one funder and hide a later funder. In the member query use the same indexable candidate + exact-filter pattern and deterministic `ORDER BY score DESC, member id` with per-type SQL limit. Keep `sqlx::query!` macro verification. Explicit transaction owns `SET LOCAL` and query.

### HTTP and UI

Change service output and `SearchEntityResponseData::from` together. Keep current entity fields and add:

```json
{"match_source":{"kind":"funder_alias","name":"West Midlands Unite"}}
```

Canonical funder results use `{ "kind": "canonical_name" }`; MPs use `{ "kind": "member_name" }`. This is a closed wire vocabulary, serialized from the Rust enum / one DTO conversion; no parallel string allowlist. TypeScript `Entity` variants carry `matchSource` as a discriminated union. `readEntities(unknown)` validates both shape and source-kind/entity-kind pairing, and rejects malformed aliases rather than defaulting. In `SearchPage.svelte`, render a secondary plain-text `Matched alias: {name}` adjacent to the canonical link; use normal text interpolation, preserve accessible reading order, and let it wrap on narrow screens. Canonical/member matches retain the current visual density.

### Tests

- PostgreSQL repository behavior: alias-only query returns canonical identity plus exact observed alias; canonical wins equal score; among equal winning aliases lexical alias is selected; alias outranks canonical when strictly higher; exact threshold boundary is included; strictness 0 still returns zero-score candidates as before; strictness 1 retains exact score matches; limits remain per funder/member type; deterministic ties. Include an EXPLAIN-focused assertion only if stable in this test DB; index correctness is primarily verified by confirming the query uses the GiST-compatible operator and migration creates the three indexes.
